[00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.900]   Greater Chongqing, the United Federation of China
[00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:13.680]   At its pre-war height, this region boasted a population of over 35 million people.
[00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.060]   Now, there are barely 50,000.
[00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:22.160]   Reconstruction funds have been slow to arrive in this part of the country,
[00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.160]   the government choosing to concentrate on more densely populated coasts.
[00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:33.020]   There is no central power grid, no running water besides the Yangtze River,
[00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:40.880]   but the streets are clear of rubble and the local security council has prevented any post-war outbreaks.
[00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.420]   The chairman of that council is Kuang Ying-chu,
[00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.420]   a medical doctor who, despite his advanced age and wartime injuries,
[00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.240]   still manages to make house calls to all his patients.
[00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:02.360]   The first outbreak I saw was in a remote village that officially had no name.
[00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.720]   The residents called it New Dachang, but this was more out of nostalgia than anything else.
[00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:14.400]   Their former home, Old Dachang, had stood since the period of the Three Kingdoms,
[00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:19.100]   with farms and houses and even trees said to be centuries old.
[00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.960]   When the Three Gorges Dam was completed and reservoir waters began to rise,
[00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:26.060]   much of Dachang was destroyed.
[00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:33.420]   Much of Dachang has been disassembled, brick by brick, then rebuilt on higher ground.
[00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:40.720]   This New Dachang, however, was not a town anymore, but a National Historic Museum.
[00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:48.200]   It must have been a heartbreaking irony for those poor peasants to see their town saved,
[00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:51.680]   but then only being able to visit it as a tourist.
[00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.400]   Maybe that is why some of them choose the name,
[00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:59.740]   to name their newly constructed hamlet New Dachang,
[00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:05.600]   to preserve some connection to their heritage, even if it was only in name.
[00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.300]   I personally didn't know that this Old Dachang existed,
[00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:14.700]   so you can imagine how confused I was when the call came in.
[00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.200]   The hospital was quiet.
[00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.920]   It had been a slow night, even for the increasing number of drunk driving accidents.
[00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.900]   Motorcycles were becoming very popular.
[00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:33.320]   We used to say that your Harley Davidsons killed more young Chinese than all the GIs in the Korean War.
[00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:37.060]   That's why I was so grateful for a quiet shift.
[00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.880]   I was tired, my back and feet ached.
[00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:46.060]   I was on my way out to smoke a cigarette and watch the dawn when I heard my name being paged.
[00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:52.180]   The receptionist that night was new and couldn't quite understand the dialect.
[00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.820]   There had been an accident, or an illness.
[00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:57.820]   It was an emergency.
[00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:59.820]   That part was obvious.
[00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.700]   And could we please send help at once?
[00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.700]   A few days later.
